[32m+[m[32m<p>The Mask Editor saves a black and white PNG for use by overlays and image modules. Enter a mask name and choose a background image with the Allsky image picker, or use the current camera image. Use the brush or filled rectangle, ellipse, and circle to draw white areas that reveal the image. Set Paint to black to hide an area with any drawing tool, or use the eraser to paint black freehand. Brush size controls brush and line width; feather softens the edge of each object. Select an object to drag it, adjust its shape, colour, size or feather, or delete it. Undo and Redo also work after moving or deleting objects. The object list shows the top object first and lets you select, hide, duplicate, or reorder shapes.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m<p><strong>Detect image circle</strong> looks for the circular image boundary against darker surroundings and adds an editable white circle with a feathered edge. Check its position before saving, since detection depends on the background image. Feathered edges appear in the editor preview as you adjust them. Background opacity changes only the background image in the editor; drawn objects and the saved mask retain their opacity. <strong>Preview result</strong> shows the background through the mask at full opacity, as it will appear when applied. Click it again to resume drawing.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m<p>Saving stores the PNG in the overlay image library and keeps the editable project, a copy of the PNG, and its background in <code>config/overlay/masks/&lt;mask-name&gt;/</code>. Closing the editor, opening another saved mask, or choosing a new background with unsaved changes asks for confirmation. Saving with an existing name asks before replacing that mask; enter a different name to save a separate mask. To edit a saved mask, select its PNG in the Image Manager and click <strong>Edit Selected Mask</strong>. PNG masks made with the old editor have no editable project and must be recreated.</p>[m
[32m+[m[32m<p>Enter the name before saving. Names may contain letters, numbers, underscores, and hyphens. Use the icon toolbar to choose drawing tools, paint colour, size, feathering, and background opacity. Zoom In, Zoom Out, and Fit change only the editor view; the PNG keeps the original image dimensions. Undo, Redo, and Clear are in the header. Undo keeps up to 40 recent edits and limits memory used by large masks. After drawing a rectangle, ellipse, circle, or line, the editor switches to Select and leaves the new shape selected so you can adjust its handles. Brush and eraser remain active for repeated strokes. Use Ctrl/Cmd+Z to undo, Ctrl/Cmd+Y or Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+Z to redo, Delete or Backspace to remove the selected object, and hold Space while dragging the canvas to pan.</p>[m
